\subtree{
  \title{Decision}
  \p{Use transparent finite data for shape syntax and morphisms after proving its presentation theorem. Input/output incidence data handles augmented cases; flagged rows are only a candidate encoding. Derived \code{DecidableEq} for syntax is not equality of quotient morphisms: a normal-form theorem or another decision procedure is needed. JSON formats, validation, and renderers follow the proved datatype. Transparent shape computation is allowed in Theory; its categorical interpretation uses sealed bridge laws.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rationale}
  \p{A serialization is an implementation of a proved presentation.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rejected}
  \p{Advertising row, cube-word, or symmetry syntax as canonical without proving that the relations are respected and equality is decided correctly.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Acceptance}
  \p{\ref{at-fd-0007}; later \ref{at-kr-0003} and any client-specific shape presentation.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Status}
  \p{provisional; exact formats are not frozen before the mathematics.}
}
